# Wrangler `.dev.vars` template — copy to `.dev.vars` for local development.
#
# `wrangler dev` (including the workerd-backed `pnpm dev` via
# `@cloudflare/vite-plugin`) auto-loads this file and exposes each entry as a
# Worker binding, mirroring `wrangler secret put` for production. Never commit
# the real `.dev.vars` — it is listed in `.gitignore`.
#
# Non-secret config (e.g. `APP_URL`) belongs in the matching `wrangler*.toml`
# `[vars]`, not here. Reserve this file for credentials that must not be
# checked in.
#
# === Distribution boundary ===============================================
# The app deploys as two Workers and **the two sets below do not overlap**.
#   request Worker (`wrangler.request.<stage>.toml`)
#     SESSION_SECRET / AI_CLIENT_TOKEN_SECRET / DIRECTORY_ROUTING_SECRET
#   state Worker (`wrangler.state.<stage>.toml`)
#     IDENTITY_MAIL_ENCRYPTION_KEY / IDENTITY_RESET_TOKEN_KEY
#
# The split is load-bearing, not tidiness. `DIRECTORY_ROUTING_SECRET` must
# never reach the state Worker: a bucket that holds it can derive its own name
# from a raw address, which is the single property the HMAC routing exists to
# remove. Symmetrically the request Worker never needs to open a sealed address
# or mint a reset token, so it does not get those two keyrings.
#
# For deployed environments, set each secret against its own config:
#   wrangler secret put SESSION_SECRET -c wrangler.request.staging.toml
#   wrangler secret put IDENTITY_RESET_TOKEN_KEY -c wrangler.state.staging.toml
#
# **Local dev is the one place the boundary is not enforced**, because both
# Workers boot from this directory and therefore read this one file. That is a
# limitation of `wrangler dev`, not a licence to merge the sets when deploying.
# =========================================================================
#
# Every value below is left empty on purpose: a secret published in this file
# would be a valid key for anyone who reads the repository. Generate your own —
# each Worker refuses to serve until its own set is present.
#
#   openssl rand -base64 48
#
# Single-value secrets need at least 32 characters. Keyring secrets are a JSON
# array of `{ generation, key, role? }`, with exactly one `active` entry (the
# default when `role` is omitted) and at most one `"previous"`, so that a
# rotation can keep reading what the old key wrote.

# --- request Worker ------------------------------------------------------

# HMAC key that signs session cookies.
SESSION_SECRET=""

# Signing key for AI client tokens (#13). Deliberately **not** the session
# secret: the two token kinds differ in TTL and in how they are revoked, so
# sharing a key would drag one rotation into the other.
AI_CLIENT_TOKEN_SECRET=""

# Keyring that HMACs a canonical address into an Identity Directory bucket
# name. Each entry additionally carries `bucketCount` — how many buckets that
# generation was split into. It cannot be defaulted: a Durable Object's name
# records the bucket *index*, not the count, so a previous generation's modulus
# is unrecoverable if it was not written down.
#
#   DIRECTORY_ROUTING_SECRET='[{"generation":1,"key":"<48+ random chars>","bucketCount":16}]'
DIRECTORY_ROUTING_SECRET=""

# --- state Worker --------------------------------------------------------

# Keyring that seals `credential_mappings.encrypted_canonical` (AES-256-GCM).
# **Signup fails without it**: a reservation whose address can never be
# recovered is worse than a refused signup, so the write refuses loudly.
#
#   IDENTITY_MAIL_ENCRYPTION_KEY='[{"generation":1,"key":"<48+ random chars>"}]'
IDENTITY_MAIL_ENCRYPTION_KEY=""

# Keyring that signs password-reset tokens. Each token row records the
# generation that signed it, so a rotation can still verify tokens in flight.
#
#   IDENTITY_RESET_TOKEN_KEY='[{"generation":1,"key":"<48+ random chars>"}]'
IDENTITY_RESET_TOKEN_KEY=""
